    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Active mutual fund managers may incur significant transaction costs and face conflict-of-interest issues, such as selling their own holdings before launching new funds or trading across their own asset-family unit, which can drag on performance. In contrast, passively managed ETFs generally have lower management fees and avoid these personal trading conflicts, though their strict adherence to index rules can lead to underperformance if the market outperforms the chosen benchmark. Additionally, while ETFs execute trades intraday, their use of authorized participant mechanisms can sometimes result in temporary liquidity premiums during periods of high volatility.",
